     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\":2,\"rationale\":\"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks exfiltration. No single control does. Why the pair works: (1) mfa_admin blocks auth_dc and auth_adfs. So admin_dc and admin_adfs can never be reached. (2) auth_db, auth_fileserver and auth_backup each require (NOT mfa_dbadmin OR admin_dc OR admin_adfs). With mfa_dbadmin deployed and no dc/adfs admin, all three are blocked. (3) The db, fileserver and backup escalations and every exfiltrate technique depend on those footholds, so exfiltrated stays false. Why no single control is enough: phishing always gives a workstation foothold and stolen_user, which also bypasses mfa_user. With mfa_dbadmin alone, the attacker reaches the internal network, gets an appserver foothold, kerberoasts stolen_admin, takes the DC, reaches admin_dc and then auth_db. With mfa_admin alone, the attacker gets an appserver/ciserver foothold, kerberoasts stolen_admin, escalates on ciserver and leaks secrets to get stolen_dbadmin, then uses auth_db. Segmented alone fails because escalate_workstation gives admin_workstation, which satisfies pivot_internal. No single patch closes all paths. So the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
